                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "44c4e46f-d036-3cc2-bd87-052a67ea99c1",
                        "entry_id": "85ba4cc2-0cb9-38d2-9efd-36ffb796009d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Teiler einer natürlichen Zahl, der selbst Primzahl ist"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "832263b9-d695-4b52-b791-d59caf18a4d8",
                                "sense_id": "44c4e46f-d036-3cc2-bd87-052a67ea99c1",
                                "text": "Jede Nicht-Primzahl lässt sich in wenigstens zwei Primfaktoren zerlegen."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9440affb-f048-4a45-958d-4d1e83ddbb91",
                                "sense_id": "44c4e46f-d036-3cc2-bd87-052a67ea99c1",
                                "text": "Primfaktoren sind immer Primzahlen."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a7f88fc9-8251-46c1-844e-069349e467d9",
                                "sense_id": "44c4e46f-d036-3cc2-bd87-052a67ea99c1",
                                "text": "„Ein internationales Forscherteam hat eine Zahl mit 307 Dezimalstellen in ihre Primfaktoren zerlegt – das ist der bisherige Weltrekord.“"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f12fdaeb-b596-42b4-8b41-455b8f958562",
                                "sense_id": "44c4e46f-d036-3cc2-bd87-052a67ea99c1",
                                "text": "„Es ist ein elementarer Satz der Zahlentheorie, daß jede natürliche Zahl in eindeutiger Weise in Primfaktoren zerlegbar ist.“"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d38216a2-1059-4a04-a329-136b04e1a42a",
                                "sense_id": "44c4e46f-d036-3cc2-bd87-052a67ea99c1",
                                "text": "„Wer alle Darstellungen einer natürlichen Zahl als Produkt von zwei oder mehr natürlichen Zahlen sucht, sollte zuallerest alle Primfaktoren finden, die in der Zahl stecken.“"
                            }
                        ],
                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "fe7b1688-fe25-4242-a719-6f7db781e260",
                        "source_entry_id": "85ba4cc2-0cb9-38d2-9efd-36ffb796009d",
                        "source_sense_id": "44c4e46f-d036-3cc2-bd87-052a67ea99c1",
                        "source_sense_description": "Mathematik: Teiler einer natürlichen Zahl, der selbst Primzahl ist",
                        "target_lang_word": "prime factor",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "prime factor",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "1a4d950b-fd50-3899-9112-52d1e4c96ffd",
                                        "entry_id": "98645dfc-541e-34b7-b003-75fb62b64c01",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A factor of a given integer which is also a prime number."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5bb1c743-2f23-4d4d-a352-febde10bc0a0",
                                                "sense_id": "1a4d950b-fd50-3899-9112-52d1e4c96ffd",
                                                "text": "Hence, the prime factors of 100 are 1, 2, 2, 5, 5, and 1 × 2 × 2 × 5 × 5 = 100. From the above illustration, we derive the following rule for finding all the prime factors of any composite number."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b075a6f2-759f-40b5-a31f-25a34a62e68c",
                                                "sense_id": "1a4d950b-fd50-3899-9112-52d1e4c96ffd",
                                                "text": "Theorem 5.5 Dickman's Theorem. The probability of a number N chosen at random having a prime factor between N#92;alpha and N#123;#92;alpha(1#43;#92;delta)#125; is approximately #61;#92;delta, independent of the magnitude of #92;alpha, if #92;delta is small."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "725c8c51-e102-4474-9a38-831b23fcf4ff",
                                                "sense_id": "1a4d950b-fd50-3899-9112-52d1e4c96ffd",
                                                "text": "If any subset of the set of prime factors of a be taken, then the product of the elements of this subset is a factor of a.\nFor example, 12 is a factor of 60 and the set of prime factors of 12 is {2, 2, 3}, which is a subset of {2, 2, 3, 5}, which is the set of prime factors of 60."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "66cc1f4c-d9ce-328e-9c4e-e9c392552857",
                                        "entry_id": "98645dfc-541e-34b7-b003-75fb62b64c01",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see prime, factor."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "61f4f4c3-5d80-41f8-be3f-bd436a75b419",
                                                "sense_id": "66cc1f4c-d9ce-328e-9c4e-e9c392552857",
                                                "text": "It became apparent to me that Mr. Genthner's open profession of being gay was the prime factor in his conviction and sentencing."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "prime factor",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "799feafd-a8a3-39fe-a3cb-91cbe00f71c3",
                                        "entry_id": "a2ab5f79-b8ab-39b6-bac6-ef00af791133",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To reduce an integer to its set of prime factors."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "transitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "980d11c8-d636-4c88-b297-3fd79e863770",
                                                "sense_id": "799feafd-a8a3-39fe-a3cb-91cbe00f71c3",
                                                "text": "Any composite number can be prime factored; that is, it can be written as a product of prime numbers (excluding 1) in one and only one way.[…]When prime factoring numbers, it is standard to rearrange the factors so that the numbers are in increasing order."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2ae43d98-6369-4daf-9963-c8e622217f95",
                                                "sense_id": "799feafd-a8a3-39fe-a3cb-91cbe00f71c3",
                                                "text": "For example, the number 30, which is not prime, can be prime factored as 2 x 3 x 5."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "039fceed-c8de-4207-88dc-ab4786f59513",
                                                "sense_id": "799feafd-a8a3-39fe-a3cb-91cbe00f71c3",
                                                "text": "Another variation of the technique for changing radicals to simplest form is to prime factor the radicand and then to look for perfect squares in exponential form."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "0cf80ed9-4173-44d9-837d-95129c3e0c26",
                        "source_entry_id": "85ba4cc2-0cb9-38d2-9efd-36ffb796009d",
